abstract: "Xie, Hyytiä and Righter (ACM SIGMETRICS Perform. Eval. Rev. 53(4) (2026) 42-48; arXiv:2509.21693v1) introduced a fluid model of size-aware dispatching and conjectured that, with two servers, the relative workload imbalance increases along every optimal path. This package solves the two-server problem exactly: for every nondegenerate mean-one size law and every load below one, one quadrature of D(h)=E|S-h| gives the optimal value, policy and path. Small jobs go to the shorter queue and large jobs to the longer one, with a cutoff that rises with the imbalance. The conjecture is proved as posed and extended to all nondegenerate laws, with a unique optimal path; it fails for deterministic sizes. The heavy-traffic limit (1-rho)V -> P(S>=m)x^2 (m the work median, atoms split) corrects a factor in the source's Proposition 3 and matches the CARD constant of Xie, Grosof and Scully (fluid limit proved; stochastic link heuristic). Further results: an exact rational controller for finite laws, certified bounds and a bin policy, Wasserstein stability, an exact Pareto enclosure and noisy-estimate bounds. A seeded simulation of the stochastic queue is numerical evidence only; no policy dominates in every cell. Unrefereed AI-assisted candidate; checked by internal AI-assisted review, two AI-assisted adversarial audits and a verification re-review only. Text and data CC0-1.0; code MIT (see LICENSES.md)."
